# Writing for a reader

This is the charter of the `expositor` and `provenance` reviewers, and the checklist for
anyone drafting a write-up that people outside the project will read. The order in which
the reviewers run is in the `gate-chain` skill.

## Why this exists

The review gates that keep findings honest (claim-auditor, red-team, the strength-word
scan) all push in one direction: against over-claiming. Nothing in them represents the
reader. A document written to survive them drifts toward a qualifier on every claim,
apparatus before it is needed, and the actual idea buried. It can be honest, true and
rigorous, and still unreadable, even to its author.

Three failure modes recur:

1. **Defence.** Text written to placate an imagined skeptic: per-claim hedges, "what this
   is not" asides, machinery dumped up front.
2. **Hype**, the overcorrection. Text written to sell: "a ... in disguise", "this is not a
   curiosity", headers that announce the surprise instead of showing it. Defence and hype
   are one disease: both write *at* a non-peer instead of *to* a peer, and both put remarks
   about the mathematics where the mathematics should be.
3. **Passing the unreadable.** A reviewer flags the section where the reader gets lost, then
   excuses it because "the reader can take it on faith and move on". That is the symptom,
   not a mitigation: an object presented without its derivation blocks understanding.

## Two documents, two jobs

| | Internal staging document | Reader-facing write-up |
|---|---|---|
| Reader | the author and the honesty reviewers | a mathematician who wants to learn and reuse |
| Records | everything, positive and negative | the settled story only |
| Reviewers | claim-auditor, red-team, scans | proof-referee, provenance, expositor, cold-reader, and one fidelity check against the staging document, by a fresh claim-auditor |
| Honesty | exhaustive, per claim; defensive is fine | carried by structure: theorem versus conjecture, plus one roadmap |

Defensive honesty belongs in the staging document. The write-up inherits only settled
status. The single **fidelity check** confirms the write-up states nothing as proved that
the staging document records as conjectural, and strengthens no theorem in translation. A
fresh `claim-auditor` does it, given both documents: the author is the reader least able to
see where a translation strengthened a claim. An
argued claim (`docs/discipline/informal-arguments.md`) keeps its label in the write-up.
{{#if modules.attempts}}
It has a second half, in the same brief: compare the write-up's account of how the result was reached with the
record ({{#if modules.conjectures}}the ledger, {{/if}}`bin/attempts --target <id>` and the work folder). Flag a reconstruction
that hides a step the record shows was hard, or claims a motivation the record contradicts.
The auditor returns it as a table: step, the record says, the write-up says.
{{/if}}

Anti-scope: a short internal note that nobody outside the project reads needs only the
honesty reviewers. When it is unclear whether people outside the project will read a
document, ask {{owner}} before choosing its reviewers.

## The rules, in the order the reader meets them

**A. Open with the reason to care.** Show what is surprising, the new way of seeing it, and
what it connects to, before the machinery. State it plainly and exhibit it in an example;
do not announce that it is striking. Give one throughline and return to it.

**B. Fix the reader and the conversation.** Name the intended reader and what they already
know well. The conversation is inquiry and teaching (hand a peer a reusable tool), not
persuasion against a skeptic. How much of each step to spell out depends on that reader.

**C. Global before local.** Give the outline, then the detail. Introduce apparatus only when
it is needed; split long arguments into lemmas. A sketch at the top does not excuse an
unmotivated derivation beneath it: the two are checked separately.

**D. Motivate every step.** Each nontrivial step, and each load-bearing claim inside it,
needs its **role** (what it does; what would break without it) and, where possible, its
**insight** (where it could have come from). Name objects in standard form ("this is a
quadratic in t", "this is a Möbius transformation") so the reader's own knowledge fires.
Before a reduction, say why it is the right reduction. Build the proof on the feature that
makes the result striking (a symmetry, a uniqueness), so the hook and the engine are the
same object.

**E. Substance over apparatus.** The payload is the reusable method, not only the theorem
headline; make it visible. Rigor is the reader seeing why each step holds, not formal
density. Give every formal object its meaning at the point it appears. "The reader can take
this on faith" raises the severity of a gap; it never lowers it.

**F. Precise, defined, justified, cited where relevant.** Use the exact standard term
(subspace, quotient) rather than a coined phrase. Define every term and symbol before first
use. Justify the easy-but-important claims and every scope or exclusion claim. Cite only
what bears on the sentence.

**G. Honesty by structure; no defence, no hype.** A claim's status sits on the claim once,
through its environment, plus a single roadmap ("Sections 2–3 are background except 3.4").
Cut contentless negations and editorial remarks about the paper's own interest. An
application motivates the object once, up front; do not re-invoke it as a refrain. Name and
value the part that is already understood (for instance the cases a symmetry forces) rather
than calling it a nuisance, and state the result at the generality it achieves.

**H. Show, don't only tell.** Examples before abstraction; let definitions arise from a
concrete question. An example does the work (computes the gcd, exhibits the factorization)
instead of asserting its conclusion, carries through to the general object, and defines a
term by the example rather than pointing ahead to a definition not yet read.

## Provenance: what is new, what is {{owner}}'s, what is classical

A separate question from readability, and a separate reviewer, so the expositor is never
tempted to strip good exposition for being "not new". For every substantive result the
`provenance` reviewer asks: new, from {{owner}}'s prior work, or classical?

- **Someone else's result:** add a citation, never cut. The reader cannot be assumed to know
  the literature; a clean exposition of it earns its place.
- **{{owner}}'s own prior work:** the intended reader is assumed to have read it, so a
  re-derivation is apparatus they already have. Here, and only here, cite it out.
- Every specific attribution ("Theorem 4.2 of …") is checked against the source text before
  it is written.

